docs:
Installed `SplitText` (`@/components/ui/split-text`, a Motion component) and the splitter it is built on (`splitText` in `@/components/ui/split-text-core`, with its helpers in `split-text-*` files next to it). Depends on `motion`.

```tsx
import { stagger } from "motion";
import { SplitText } from "@/components/ui/split-text";

<SplitText
  options={{ type: "chars,words,lines", mask: "lines" }}
  variants={{
    hidden: { chars: { opacity: 0, y: "100%" } },
    visible: {
      chars: ({ lineIndex }) => ({
        opacity: 1,
        y: "0%",
        transition: { delay: stagger(0.02, { startDelay: lineIndex * 0.15 }) },
      }),
    },
  }}
  initial="hidden"
  animate="visible"
>
  <h1>Per-line staggered reveal</h1>
</SplitText>
```

- Wrap exactly one element. Its text is split on the client, after `document.fonts.ready`; the wrapper stays hidden until then, so render it in a client component.
- Character splits keep the font's kerning: each character gets a margin that restores the pair spacing the browser had before the split. Ligatures are turned off for character splits, since a ligature cannot span two boxes.
- Without Motion, use the splitter directly: `splitText(element, { type: "words" })` from `@/components/ui/split-text-core` returns `{ chars, words, lines, revert }`. Set `dir="auto"` (or the right direction) on the element yourself in a right-to-left page.
- Lines are grouped by where the split words land, and words break at spaces and dashes only: a word the browser hyphenated, a long URL under `overflow-wrap`, or Japanese with no spaces does not wrap the same way once split. For text like that, use Split Lines (`split-lines`), which reads lines off the paint.
- Reduced motion is honoured by default (`reducedMotion="user"`).

Ported from Griffo by dimi (dimicx), MIT; the license notice is at the end of every file. Kugiri by Edoardo Lunardi (S-383), the alternative splitter compared in the fidelity notes, is ported separately as Split Lines.

